!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/temporal.tn?sha256=b02b15330dac960140a47eb081928ee14e82dbda87a057eceec9441451ff5cbd"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/meta.tn?sha256=a59a053ce96af5642718fc37f9271476fef9489fef7bd4cce5341ea2c79c9d6e"
@doc:"""
  TSON temporal — the temporal type library (2026 Revision 39 draft).

  Its root holds the temporal types a data schema writes: `date`, `time`,
  `datetime`, `duration` and `period`. Their constructors are machinery,
  members of `temporal`, a schema map nested in its root, each named by its
  path: `temporal.date_type`, `temporal.time_type`, `temporal.datetime_type`,
  `temporal.duration_type` and `temporal.period_type`.

  It imports the kernel, meta.tn, alone: the kernel's constructors,
  `numeric.count` and classifying annotations are what it builds on, and it
  needs nothing of numeric.tn or net.tn. A schema
  importing it reserves `temporal` and its root names beside the kernel's
  ([TSON-SCHEMA] §2.2.3), and sees none of numeric.tn's: importing temporal.tn
  gives `date` and no `int32`. core.tn imports it with numeric.tn and net.tn,
  for a schema that wants every bundled type.

  Hash pins here are real digests over the bytes of this copy; see meta.tn's
  note.

  An annotation never changes a value, its type, or whether it is valid:
  validation is over values, and a schema with every annotation erased admits
  exactly the same values. A value space is described without reference to
  any encoding, and where a doc mentions how TSON text spells a value it says
  so by name.

  Every constructor here binds to a governing specification, RFC 3339: it
  composes with the kernel's `atom.specification` mixin and pins the
  spec field with `=`.

  A bound or a step is of the constrained family and is typed by that
  family's instance at this document's root: a `date_type` bound is a `date`,
  a `period_type` step a `period`. A precision counts digits and is typed by
  the kernel's `numeric.count`. The bounds are field groups
  ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.11): one inclusive-or-exclusive group per side, so an
  inclusive and an exclusive bound on the same side is unrepresentable.
  Value-level coherence (the lower bound not exceeding the upper) is a
  schema-load check.

  `multiple_of`, on `temporal.duration_type` and `temporal.period_type`,
  follows the one rule numeric.tn states for every step: strictly positive,
  sign-blind, tightened by a refinement only to an integer multiple of the
  inherited step, and composed with every other facet present.

  References:
    RFC 3339              Internet date/time profile; Appendix A is
                          the duration/period grammar (ISO 8601-1:2019
                          is the informative source behind it)
    ISO/IEC 11404:2007    General Purpose Datatypes
    W3C XSD Part 2        Facet model and constraint vocabulary
  """
!schema {
  @doc:"The temporal constructors, each named by its path, `temporal.date_type`."
  temporal => !schema {
    @doc:"Date constraint vocabulary. Instance is `date`."
    date_type => meta.atom & atom.specification & {
      spec?: = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3339"
      ( min: date | exclusive_min: date )?
      ( max: date | exclusive_max: date )?
    }

    @doc:"""
      Time constraint vocabulary. Instance is `time`. `precision` is a
      constraint on the value, not on a spelling: `precision: N` admits a value
      that is a whole number of 10^-N seconds, so `precision: 3` is millisecond
      resolution and `precision: 0` whole seconds. The atom is exact — nothing
      is truncated; a value off the grid is rejected ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.5).
      Reading admits any spelling of an admitted value, trailing zeros included,
      so `12:00:00.500` is admitted under `precision: 1`; a text encoding
      writing the value writes at most N fractional digits, `12:00:00.5`.
      """
    time_type => meta.atom & atom.specification & {
      spec?:      = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3339"
      ( min: time | exclusive_min: time )?
      ( max: time | exclusive_max: time )?
      precision?: numeric.count
    }

    @doc:"""
      Datetime constraint vocabulary. Instance is `datetime`.
      `precision` as for `time_type`: the value is a whole number of 10^-N
      seconds, a constraint on the value ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.5).
      """
    datetime_type => meta.atom & atom.specification & {
      spec?:      = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3339"
      ( min: datetime | exclusive_min: datetime )?
      ( max: datetime | exclusive_max: datetime )?
      precision?: numeric.count
    }

    @doc:"""
      Elapsed-time constraint vocabulary. Instance is `duration`.
      Lexical form is the RFC 3339 Appendix A `duration` production with three
      extensions and one restriction: an optional leading `-`; a fraction on the
      seconds component only, written with `.` (`PT0.5S`, never `PT1.5H` or
      `PT0,5S`); any subset of D, H, M, S may be omitted provided at least one
      component is present and order is kept (`PT1H30S` is admitted); and the Y
      and month-M components are not admitted — `P1M` is one month and belongs
      to `period`; a minute is `PT1M`. `PnW` stands alone, as in the ABNF
      (`P1W2D` is an error). A day is exactly 86400 s and a week 7 days.

      The value is a signed exact decimal number of seconds — numeric.tn's
      `number`'s value space, in seconds: nothing is rounded. So `PT90M`, `PT1H30M` and
      `P0DT5400S` are one value, `PT0S`, `P0D` and `-PT0S` are one value, and
      ordering is TOTAL. Bounds compare the value, not the token.

      Both ends of that space are fixed at a signed 64-bit count of nanoseconds.
      The seconds component admits at most nine fractional digits, and a
      magnitude may not exceed 2^63 - 1 nanoseconds (about 292 years) — stated
      as a magnitude, so negating an admitted duration always yields an admitted
      one. A processor MUST represent every value in that range and MUST reject
      one outside it whether or not its own representation could hold it.

      `precision` as for `time_type`, and it is `fraction_digits` on the seconds
      count: the value is a whole number of 10^-N seconds, so `precision: 0`
      admits whole seconds only and `precision: 9` is the nanosecond grid. A
      constraint on the value and not on a spelling — `PT0.50S` is a whole
      number of tenths — and it may not exceed 9. `multiple_of` is
      `numeric.decimal_type`'s `multiple_of` on that same count, under the
      header's uniform rule: `PT15M` admits only quarter-hour values.
      """
    duration_type => meta.atom & atom.specification & {
      spec?:       = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3339#appendix-A"
      ( min: duration | exclusive_min: duration )?
      ( max: duration | exclusive_max: duration )?
      precision?:  numeric.count
      multiple_of?: duration
    }

    @doc:"""
      Calendar-span constraint vocabulary. Instance is `period`. Lexical
      form is the RFC 3339 Appendix A `duration` production restricted to an
      optional leading `-`, then `P` followed by a Y component, an M component,
      or both in that order; no fraction, no W or D component and no `T` part
      (`P1M15D`, `P1Y0D` and `P1YT1H` are errors — use `duration`, or a record
      with a `period` field and a `duration` field, for mixed spans). The value
      is a signed integer number of months, so `P1Y` and `P12M` are equal,
      `P0Y`, `P0M` and `-P0M` are equal, and ordering is TOTAL. Bounds compare
      the value, not the token. `multiple_of` is a period under the header's
      uniform rule: `P3M` admits only whole quarters.
      """
    period_type => meta.atom & atom.specification & {
      spec?:       = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3339#appendix-A"
      ( min: period | exclusive_min: period )?
      ( max: period | exclusive_max: period )?
      multiple_of?: period
    }
  }

  @doc:"Calendar date. RFC 3339 full-date YYYY-MM-DD."
  @atom.ordering:TOTAL @atom.bounded:true
  date => !temporal.date_type {}

  @doc:"""
    Time of day. RFC 3339 full-time HH:MM:SS[.s…] with a mandatory offset. The
    value is the time of day in UTC, on the finite interval [00:00:00,
    24:00:00), so `10:00:00+01:00` and `09:00:00Z` are one value, and ordering
    is TOTAL. Normalising to UTC wraps at midnight (`23:30:00-02:00` is
    `01:30:00Z`): a local time of day — "09:00 wherever you are" — is not this
    type. Equality and bounds compare the value. The offset is a spelling: TSON
    text preserves it as written, an encoding that stores the instant may drop
    it. A leap second, `23:59:60`, which RFC 3339's grammar admits, lies outside
    the interval and is refused.
    """
  @atom.ordering:TOTAL @atom.bounded:true
  time => !temporal.time_type {}

  @doc:"""
    Date and time. RFC 3339 date-time with a mandatory offset. The value is the
    instant on the UTC timeline, so `2026-01-01T10:00:00+01:00` and
    `2026-01-01T09:00:00Z` are one value, and ordering is TOTAL. An offset of
    `-00:00` (offset unknown, RFC 3339 §4.3) is the same instant as `Z` and
    compares as such. A local datetime — a wall clock with no offset — is not
    this type. Equality and bounds compare the value. The offset is a spelling:
    TSON text preserves it as written, an encoding that stores the instant may
    drop it, and a value for which the offset is data — an appointment shown in
    its booked zone — is a different value space, not this type. A leap second,
    second 60, which RFC 3339's grammar admits, is not an instant on that
    timeline and is refused.
    """
  @atom.ordering:TOTAL @atom.bounded:true
  datetime => !temporal.datetime_type {}

  @doc:"""
    Elapsed time — a signed exact decimal number of seconds, within the range
    `duration_type` fixes. No Y or month-M component, so every value has a fixed
    length and ordering is TOTAL. `P1M` is a month and belongs to `period`; a
    minute is `PT1M`. A day is exactly 86400 s and a week 7 days, so `PT90M`,
    `PT1H30M` and `P0DT5400S` are one value.
    """
  @atom.ordering:TOTAL @atom.bounded:true
  duration => !temporal.duration_type {}

  @doc:"""
    Calendar span — a signed integer number of months. `P1Y` and `P12M` are one
    value. A span that is genuinely both is a record with a field of each.
    """
  @atom.ordering:TOTAL @atom.bounded:false
  period => !temporal.period_type {}
}